## Goal
Create a sample-level PCA from the project gene counts to visualize sample similarity, with points coloured by the recorded treated versus untreated condition.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. In `gars-bio`, use Python with pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn, and matplotlib. Read the resolved gene counts table, use gene identifiers as row labels, and use its sample columns as the expression matrix.
2. Apply `log2(count + 1)` to the length-scaled counts; select the 500 genes with greatest variance across samples (or all genes if fewer than 500), using deterministic variance ranking. Center and scale each selected gene across samples, setting zero-variance scaled values to zero.
3. Run scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)` on samples by selected genes, with `random_state=0`. Join sample labels from the resolved design by exact `sample_id`; color points by `condition` (`treated` and `untreated`), with sample IDs labelled.
4. Save the two principal component coordinates and explained variance fractions as a CSV, and save a labelled scatter plot as a PNG. Read inputs only; write outputs under `results/`. Execution is in the batch job.

